6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: D. Hool

In the Book of the Duchess Chaucer is working in a tight couplet/ tail-rhyme dream vision and he does in fact put all of the other items to work as rhyme words.

* wente is the regular past of *wen* / *go* and is rhymed e.g. with sente/rente in the narrative couplets;
* here is a normal line-final rhyme word, e.g. here / dere / there;
* fool is a normal line-final rhyme word, e.g. fool / cool;
* countour, Middle English *countour* ‘counter’, is used in the poem and is rhymed with the -our class, e.g. countour / tretour.

*hool* is not a rhyme partner that Chaucer ever sets up in the Duchess. The rhyme that is normally pointed out for fool is fool ~ cool, not fool ~ hool, and the form *hool* does not occur as a rhyming line-ending in the poem. The word is therefore the one of the list that is not put into a Chaucerian rhyme in the Book of the Duchess.

2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: F. None of the above

All five of the forms are actually used by Chaucer in rhyming position in The Book of the Duchess and they are all rhymed with a partner in the poem. 

* wente  is the normal ME past of ‘to go’ and is put into the rhyme royal octave, e.g. wente / sente, kente etc.;
* here is regularly used at line-end and rhymed with there / where / were etc.;
* fool and hool are used as a rhyming pair in the poem – ME hool / hoole ‘whole, hale’ is the regular rhyme for fool / cool, so both words are actually rhymed with one another;
* countour, ME ‘counter’, is likewise put into the rhyme scheme of the Duchess and rhymed with its conventional partners.

Hence none of A–E is a word left un-rhymed in the work.

